why 2 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION A cadence in E minor where every resolution falls: soprano F♯–E, the alto's D♯ drops to B, the seventh A to G and the bass to a low E.
why 3 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano (B–C♯) and tenor (D–C♯) meet on C♯ by step from opposite sides. The raised C♯ belongs to the rising melodic minor.
why 4 1/4 OVERLAP The bass leaps up a fourth to C, above where the tenor was, while the tenor leaps up to E. They move up together, so the order stays clear.
why 6 0 SIMILAR_MOTION The same cadence as in bar 2: every voice falls into E minor.
why 7 3/4 DOUBLING Soprano (C–B) and tenor (A–B) meet on B by step from opposite sides.
why 8 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION A cadence on G (III): soprano A–G, the alto's F♯ drops to D, the seventh C to B and the bass to G. All the resolutions point down.
why 9 0 DOUBLING The tune leaps up to B while the tenor keeps its B from the fermata chord. The doubled third keeps the held note.
why 9 1/4 DOUBLING A passing chord over the bass's step F♯; its doubled B is the tenor's held note.
why 9 1/4 SIX_FOUR The bass walks down G–F♯–E under these chords; the one over F♯ is a passing ⁶₄ on that line.
why 9 3/4 DIRECT The tune falls D–B while the bass steps F♯–E: a hidden fifth softened by the step in the bass.
why 11 0 SIMILAR_MOTION A half cadence on B where every voice falls: soprano C♯–B, the seventh E to D♯, the tenor's A♯ to F♯ and the bass to B.
why 12 3/8 UNRESOLVED_NCT The bass skips E–C–A♯–B, a winding approach to the dominant. Its C is a passing note between the tonic and the A♯ of V of V.
why 12 1/2 CROSS_RELATION The bass's C, then C♯ in the tenor: Bach turns sharply toward the dominant's dominant. The C♯ is the third of F♯⁷, so the ear hears it as part of a new chord.
why 12 1/2 LEAPS The bass falls E–C–A♯ before resolving to B. Its ends form a tritone, but A♯ is a leading tone that resolves at once.
why 13 0 SIMILAR_MOTION The piece ends on E major, a Picardy third. Every voice falls into it: the alto's D♯ drops to B, the seventh A to G♯ and the bass to a deep E.
